            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a pact, then the Latin for to bargain for."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: violō, violāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "violō, violāre — \"to violate, to break; to wrong\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hostēs foedus violāvērunt. The enemy broke the treaty."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "violō, violāre, violāvī, violātum, kin to vīs, force. Fidem violāre, to break one's word. English violate and violence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "violō is built on vīs \"force\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): to treat with force. English violate and violence come from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
